=F0=9F=94=8D The missing layer in AI coding: runtime validation
Greptile sends scoped agents into sandboxes, tests suspected failures, and =
returns screenshots and logs inside the PR.
Stay updated with today=E2=80=99s top AI news, papers, and repos.
Signup
|
Work With Us
|
Follow on X
Hey,
Your AI coding assistant writes a clean pull request, the types pass, and t=
he logic looks correct. Then the application freezes at startup, an async e=
dge case triggers a 500 error, or a shell bypass exposes secrets that no te=
xt-only review caught.
Greptile is targeting this validation gap with TREX, a runtime execution la=
yer that runs PR branches inside isolated sandboxes and returns concrete pr=
oof of failure. Here is why AI code review needs to move from predicting bu=
gs to executing the code.
Today's Author
Ben Dickson is a veteran software engineer and former CTO known as the "Eng=
ineer's Journalist" for translating technical AI research into practical, p=
roduction-focused insights. He is a lead contributor to TechCrunch and Vent=
ureBeat.
Technical Deep Dive
Why AI code review needs proof, not predictions
AI coding assistants can generate massive volumes of code in seconds, but w=
hen a text-only review misses a subtle runtime error, developers pay a mass=
ive debugging tax, spending hours scraping logs, scrambling to reproduce en=
vironments, and writing urgent hotfixes under pressure.
Greptile's new tool,
TREX
, tackles this bottleneck by moving from static code analysis to active run=
time execution.
TREX actively runs PR branches in a dynamic sandbox and automatically spins=
 up dev servers, mocks API inputs, and executes interactions to catch failu=
res that static analysis cannot see.
By reading the code and then running it,
Greptile
mimics the review and QA pipelines that real engineering teams build and au=
tomates the gruntwork of validation.
By executing the code during validation, TREX catches critical issues that =
static analysis misses:
Runtime bugs like the application inexplicably freezing two seconds into st=
artup.
Complex security vulnerabilities, like a shell expansion bypass that quietl=
y leaks secrets.
The ceiling of static analysis
Though large language models have gotten much better at finding bugs and re=
asoning about code logic, they still struggle to predict complex runtime st=
ate failures.
These are issues caused by dynamic memory mutations, asynchronous event loo=
ps, race conditions, or stateful database connection pools that happen duri=
ng code execution.
This is exactly what creates the debugging tax. A text-only AI reviewer wil=
l frequently approve a PR where the TypeScript types and function logic loo=
k clean in isolation. However, in production, an edge-case null payload pas=
sing through an async middleware chain triggers an unhandled promise reject=
ion and a 500 server error.
When a bug like this slips past review, the team is forced into damage cont=
rol: bisecting git commits, scraping distributed log aggregators, and patch=
ing the logic under immense pressure.
Safely scaling AI code generation requires an evolutionary leap in QA. Team=
s need to shift from passive compute prediction to active code execution. A=
ctive execution requires the AI to actually compile, execute, and evaluate =
the software in an isolated environment.
Instead of an LLM passively guessing that a database query might time out i=
f a user ID is null, active code execution spins up an ephemeral container.=
 It fires a payload against the live endpoint, measures execution, and inte=
rcepts the real HTTP 504 log directly.
TREX runtime validation and proof of work
Attempting to make a single AI agent run services, take screenshots, and ru=
n tests can overload the model's context window.
To keep the context focused, the main Greptile reviewer acts as an orchestr=
ator. When it reads a diff and identifies an issue, it spins up a dedicated=
 TREX sub-agent scoped exclusively to investigate that specific problem in =
an isolated sandbox.
Take a new UI component hidden behind an authentication gate as an example.=
 Testing this locally normally requires setting up the dev environment, han=
dling web tokens, and manually toggling feature flags.
A TREX sub-agent figures this all out autonomously. It interacts with the a=
pp and returns to the PR with a screenshot of the rendered feature that sho=
ws whether it works or breaks.
An AI that only leaves a text comment reading "this might fail" creates mor=
e work. The developer still has to pull the branch, start the server, and v=
erify the results.
By attaching concrete artifacts to the PR, TREX ensures the developer never=
 has to run the code themselves.
Under the hood: the semantic code graph and continuous learning
Executing code in a sandbox sounds simple, but running blind tests on a com=
plex codebase is computationally impossible. Before TREX can execute a sing=
le test, it needs to know what to test.
That context comes from Greptile's underlying
Semantic Code Graph
. Before running any code, Greptile parses the entire repository to extract=
 and map directories, files, functions, variables, and classes.
When a PR is submitted, Greptile acts as an orchestrator. It deploys a swar=
m of parallel AI agents that query the graph index to trace dependencies an=
d assess impact well beyond the changed lines.
However, mapping static code isn't enough on its own. Greptile continuously=
 learns and adapts to the team's undocumented preferences and engineering s=
tandards.
By ingesting internal documentation and reading historical and ongoing PR c=
omments, the system dynamically adapts its custom rules over time. This lea=
ds to highly tailored reviews that understand the nuances of your specific =
stack.
This combination of deep codebase mapping and continuous cultural learning =
is why Greptile's core reading layer catches the subtle issues that diff-on=
ly AI tools miss:
Cross-service logical bugs
Silent exception swallowing
Stylistic regressions
The central validation layer: Greptile CLI and the `/greploop`
Greptile allows you to get the rigorous code auditing without leaving your =
existing workflows.
Developers don't have to wait for a GitHub PR to get codebase-aware feedbac=
k. The
Greptile CLI
allows engineers to run a full repository review locally in the terminal.
The CLI includes an "--agent" mode that lets your local coding assistants t=
rigger Greptile themselves. They receive findings and suggested fixes as ra=
w text to iterate on immediately.
For ultimate autonomy, the "/greploop" skill creates a self-healing develop=
er loop that follows a five-step cycle:
An AI agent writes code
It triggers a Greptile review
The agent reads the resulting feedback
It applies the exact fixes and pushes the commit
It triggers another review
The system repeats the loop up to five times to prevent runaway cycles. It =
continues until Greptile scores the PR a perfect five out of five with zero=
 unresolved comments.
Real-world impact
Brex integrated Greptile
to navigate their massive, 8-year-old monorepo with high precision and low =
noise. By catching what human reviewers easily miss, the platform helped Br=
ex ship features 30% faster. CTO James Reggio officially called it "the onl=
y AI reviewer that doesn't annoy the s**t out of me."
We can also see TREX runtime validation
catching bugs in the wild
. In the "workos/oagen" repository, Greptile caught a correctness bug leaki=
ng sibling models and breaking a selected-only contract.
More critically, in the "hashgraph-online/hol-guard" repository, it identif=
ied a severe vulnerability where a shell expansion bypass allowed secrets t=
o exfiltrate past a kubectl guard.
Crucially, TREX didn't just leave a text warning about the security flaw. I=
t actively spun up a sandbox, executed the malicious payload, fired it agai=
nst the logic, and attached the exact failure output as proof of work direc=
tly in the PR.
Greptile's benchmarks show that teams relying on this automated validation =
layer merge pull requests up to 4x faster and catch 3x more production-bloc=
king bugs than those relying on static review alone.
Enterprise-scale deployment
To support large-scale engineering teams, Greptile also offers self-hosted,=
 air-gapped deployments. Organizations can deploy the system entirely withi=
n their own virtual private cloud to maintain strict data residency require=
ments.
The platform is SOC 2 Type II compliant and includes standard enterprise co=
ntrols like SSO and comprehensive audit logs.
AI code generation is only as fast as your validation pipeline. Relying on =
manual human testing to validate AI-generated code nullifies the velocity g=
ains of using AI in the first place.
